Sure! To update a `break` node’s label to `"loopEnd"` (but only if it isn’t already `"loopEnd"`), you’ll need to pass the node’s current representation and the new label to the `updateBreak` function.

Here’s how you can do it:

```javascript
// Suppose you have the node as a JSON string (or any stringified JS object)
const nodeString = /* your node string here */;

// Call the updateBreak function
const result = updateBreak({
  node: nodeString,
  label: "loopEnd"
});

console.log(result); // This will contain the updated node (or the original if no change was needed)
```

**What you need to provide:**

1. **`node`** – A string representation of the break node you want to update.  
   Example: `"{\"type\":\"BreakStatement\",\"label\":\"oldLabel\"}"`

2. **`label`** – The new label you want to set, which is `"loopEnd"` in this case.

